<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  the About screen: what this is, what it runs, what it was read against, and what it is under.

  **the frame and nothing else.** the composition is `part_about_body.xml`, which has a `-land`
  variant, because the one thing that changes with the shape of the screen is whether the drawing sits
  above the text or beside it.

  **the toolbar is laid over the content rather than above it**, and that is what lets the drawing
  reach full height. stacked in a column, the content begins at the toolbar's bottom edge and every
  container between the two clips there, so the topmost few pixels of the drawing were sliced flat -
  and the drawing's own file has ink in its second row, so there is nothing to spare. here the scroll
  view fills the window and is padded down by the toolbar's height instead: the content still starts
  below the toolbar, and `clipToPadding` being off lets what overflows draw up into that band.

  **the toolbar carries no background of its own, which is the other half of it.** a surface behind it
  would hide exactly what this arrangement exists to show. the cost is that anything scrolled up would
  pass behind the title rather than under a solid band, and it is a cost worth naming rather than a
  free lunch - this page is written to fit without scrolling on the screens it is built for, and the
  scroll view below is insurance for a shorter one.

  **the scroll view sets `fillViewport`**, which is what lets the composition centre in the space
  rather than hang from the top of it, and a screen too short for the composition still scrolls.

  **the whole composition is lifted 8dp, and it is a translation for the reason the drawing's own lift
  is one.** it reads a little high on purpose: the toolbar carries no background and no band of its
  own, so a page centred by arithmetic sits low to an eye that takes the title's line as the top of the
  page rather than the panel's edge.

  **padding cannot do it here, and that is a measurement rather than a preference.** side by side the
  page is very nearly the height of the space under the toolbar - some 15px of slack on a landscape
  handheld - so leaving more room below than above recentres it by whatever slack is left and then
  pushes it into scrolling instead of moving it further. 16dp of extra bottom padding bought 5px of the
  8dp asked for. a translation moves what is drawn and nothing that is measured, so the lift is exactly
  the number written on it, whatever the panel.

  **the bottom padding is what pays for the translation on a screen short enough to scroll.** there the
  page hangs from the top rather than centring, and the last 8dp of it would sit past the end of the
  scroll; the 16dp below absorbs that with room to spare.

  **the attribute is on the body's own root and not on the `include` below**, because an include
  carries only `id`, `visibility` and `layout_*` across to the root it names and drops everything else
  in silence - a translation written here compiles, draws, and moves nothing.
-->
<FrameLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:background="?attr/colorSurface">

    <androidx.core.widget.NestedScrollView
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:clipChildren="false"
        android:clipToPadding="false"
        android:fillViewport="true"
        android:paddingTop="?attr/actionBarSize"
        android:paddingBottom="16dp"
        android:scrollbarStyle="outsideOverlay">

        <include
            android:id="@+id/body"
            layout="@layout/part_about_body"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="match_parent" />

    </androidx.core.widget.NestedScrollView>

    <!-- last, so it draws over the content it is laid on. -->
    <com.google.android.material.appbar.MaterialToolbar
        android:id="@+id/toolbar"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
        android:layout_gravity="top"
        app:navigationIcon="@drawable/ic_back" />

</FrameLayout>
